Version: 1.0
desc: Byte Me (reSID port) by chmaha
tags: instrument
author: chmaha
changelog:
  Initial release.
about:
  Commodore 64 SID chip synth for making chiptunes straight from a piano roll: three voices
  with the real 6581 or 8580 sound, hard sync, ring mod and the analog filter.
  Lead mode stacks all three voices on one note (sync and ring always pair up); Poly plays
  three-note chords (Poly x2 / x3 stack two or three voices per note, one per voice sound, for
  thick detuned or synced poly patches); Multi puts MIDI channels 1-3 on voices 1-3 so one instance plays a
  whole bass, lead and drums tune (channel 10 also goes to voice 3, channel 4 plays digi drums).
  Each voice has a 16-step table (waveform, note, pulse-width and cutoff steps, with a release
  section) that runs at the C64's 50 Hz frame rate like real SID music players, or locked to the
  project tempo. Hold a chord in Lead or Multi mode and one voice arpeggiates it (up, down,
  up-down, played or random, over up to four octaves, with a gate). A built-in drum kit maps GM
  drum notes to SID noise drums or to 4-bit volume-register digi drums.
  Extra SIDs: a detuned second chip for wide stereo, or up to four chips for 6, 9 or 12-voice
  poly spread across the stereo field (silent chips stop clocking to save CPU); chorus and
  stereo delay.
  User bank of 128 slots: STORE the current sound, INIT, rename; saved with the project and
  in REAPER presets.
  SID emulation ported from reSID by Dag Lem
  https://github.com/libsidplayfp/resid (GPLv2+)
  Player, drum kits, digi samples, effects and factory patches are original to this plugin.
  JSFX version released under GPLv3+ license
  Claude generated some of this code

